      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Childhood asthma can have a range of outcomes in adulthood."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To identify clinical features and exposures associated with persistence and severity of childhood asthma in adulthood."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Eighty-five of 121 subjects previously enrolled in a study of immunotherapy for childhood allergic asthma (age 5-12 years) were re-evaluated with allergy skin testing, spirometry, and interviews about asthma symptoms and medications. These young adults (age 17-30 years; 74% male) all had moderate to severe childhood asthma. Adult asthma severity was scored by using a modified version of National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute severity categories."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Thirteen (15.3%) of 85 adult subjects were in remission despite persistent childhood asthma. Another 19 subjects (22.4%) had only intermittent asthma. The remaining 53 had persistent asthma, of whom 12 (14.1%) had mild asthma, 25 (29.4%) had moderate asthma, and 16 (18.8%) had severe asthma. Subjects in remission, compared with subjects with intermittent or persistent asthma, had lower total serum IgE in childhood (412 ng/mL vs 1136 ng/mL vs 968 ng/mL; P = .02) and fewer positive allergy skin tests (7 vs 9 vs 10 from panel of 18; P = .02). Subjects in remission also had milder childhood asthma, indicated by lower average daily medication usage scores (1.6 vs 3.5 vs 4.4; P = .005) and lower percentage of days on inhaled corticosteroids (13.7% vs 24.7% vs 40.9%; P = .008). No significant association was found between current asthma severity and childhood immunotherapy ( P = .46)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The prognosis of childhood allergic asthma in adulthood is largely determined early in life. The degree of atopy appears to be a critical determinant of asthma persistence."
        }
      ]},
